 The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
 
+## Account security
+
+Account settings are available at `/devtools-times/account/settings`, linked from
+“My account” in the header. Sign in with an existing reader username and password
+to add, rename, or remove passkeys. Password authentication uses the existing
+Times authentication service; successful authentication creates a local, rotated
+HTTP-only session. Registered passkeys can also be used on the sign-in page.
+
+Passkey registration and authentication verify WebAuthn challenges, origin,
+relying-party ID, and user verification on the server. Management endpoints scope
+every operation to the authenticated reader and relying party. Passkey labels are
+account metadata, independent of the authenticator's user display name.
+
+Use Node.js 22.13 or newer. Credentials are stored in SQLite at
+`.data/passkeys.sqlite`. Set `PASSKEY_DATABASE_PATH` to a persistent volume path
+for deployments, and retain the filesystem-backed Astro session directory.
+Multiple instances need a shared credential/session store before scaling out.
+`WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN` can specify the public HTTPS origin behind a reverse proxy;
+otherwise the request origin is used. Passkeys require HTTPS (or localhost).
+
+Supported browsers receive the complete accepted credential list after loading
+the authenticated settings page and after registration or removal. Unsupported
+browsers and failed updates show instructions to manage the saved credential in
+the device's password manager. A successful signal means the update was sent;
+the authenticator controls when it applies it.
+
 ## Tech Stack
 
 - **[Astro](https://astro.build/)**: The web framework for building content-driven websites.
